  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nAn upper trough is currently over the northeastern CONUS into \nportions of the eastern CONUS. Upper ridging continues over the \nPlains into the western CONUS, keeping our area in northwesterly \nflow aloft. A shortwave trough is sliding across southern \nSaskatchewan, pushing a cold front into portions of the Northern \nPlains. Widely scattered showers are ongoing in far southeast \nKS and eastern Oklahoma where an area of 850-700 mb WAA \ncontinues. As we move into tonight and Tuesday morning, mostly \ndry conditions are expected before a slight chance of rain is \npossible to clip central KS on Tuesday morning. Better chances \nwill remain in northwestern and western Kansas. Low temperatures\nwill drop into the upper 60s to around 70. \n\nFor Tuesday, high temperatures will once again rise into the low to \nmid 90s across our area. As we move towards late Tuesday afternoon \nand night, rain chances will increase as the deeper shortwave trough \nmoves into the Upper Mississippi Valley, pushing the aforementioned \nweak cold front farther south into Nebraska. Additional storm \ndevelopment is expected off the High Plains and to propagate \neast/southeast into Kansas. Strong to severe storms are likely with \nthis activity given sufficient instability, around 25-30 kts of \neffective shear, and decent DCAPE values. The main hazards with the \nstrongest activity will be damaging wind gusts, hail up to quarter \nsize, and locally heavy rainfall. Activity may linger into the \novernight hours with chances decreasing towards Wednesday morning. \n\nFor Wednesday and Thursday, the upper air pattern will generally \nremain unchanged with upper ridging over the western CONUS and the \nRockies, keeping the Plains in northwesterly flow aloft. This will \nallow additional embedded shortwaves to eject out into the region, \nwhich will result in slight rain chances both Wednesday and Thursday \nnight. The better chances will remain off to our west, though there \nis a 20% chance for activity to clip our western fringes. For \ntemperatures, highs are anticipated to be near normal with values in \nthe upper 80s to lower 90s. Low temperatures are forecast to be in \nthe mid to upper 60s Wednesday and Thursday night. \n\nFor Friday and beyond, the upper ridge axis is progged to shift east \ntowards the Plains which will result in a warming trend starting \nFriday into the weekend. High temperatures are forecast to be in the \n90s across the area on Friday before a return to triple digit heat \non Saturday and Sunday. Low temperatures will warm a bit with values \nin the low to mid 70s. Mostly dry conditions are expected into the \nweekend with the next chance for rain possible on Sunday night into \nMonday as a deeper shortwave trough is progged to move into the \nNorthern Plains.",
